<h2 id="ranking">The craft ranking (155 cards real decks run)</h2>

<p class="sub"><strong>How to read each column.</strong> <em>Rank</em> is the sort position. <em>Card</em> is the card name (bold = you don't own a playset yet). <em>CI</em> is "color identity", which colors the card uses; "C" means colorless (any deck can use it). <em>Rarity</em> tells you which wildcard pile to spend from. <em>Set</em> is the 3-letter code of the expansion the card is in. <em>Type</em> is creature, artifact, land, etc. <em>MV</em> is mana value (what it costs to play). <em>Owned</em> is how many copies you currently have on Arena. <em>WC to 4-of</em> is how many wildcards it would cost to finish a playset. <em>Paper $</em> is what ONE copy costs in paper at its cheapest non-foil printing, and it is never discounted for what you own on Arena, because an Arena copy is not a paper copy. <strong>Both the card and its price are links.</strong> Clicking the card opens every printing of it on Scryfall sorted cheapest first, so the top row is the printing this figure was read from. The price opens that card on TCGplayer, where you can buy it. A card with no paper printing reads <em>n/a</em> and links nowhere, because there is nothing to sell. <em>Decks that run it</em> is the headline number: if you craft this card, how many of the 55 published Mardu-color Standard decklists in this project's harvest actually run it. The counts are read from the lists themselves and range from 2 to 18 across these rows, topping out at Godless Shrine on 18. The source is the creator-video harvest described in the Method note, not Untapped's own per-deck lists, which this project has not decoded. Quantity does not matter, one copy or four, the question is only whether each deck plays the card. A higher number means crafting it helps more of your decks, so each wildcard does more work. Every row here is run by at least 2 of the 55, which is the threshold for appearing in the table at all. Each row now carries a <strong>Decks</strong> button next to that count. It opens the actual lists behind the number: which harvested decks run the card, who built each one, how many copies its list runs, what the whole deck costs in rare and mythic wildcards, and a link to the video the list came from. The button reads the same pool the count was measured from, so the list it opens always has exactly as many decks as the number beside it. Switch to <strong>All Standard decks</strong> above and the buttons re-scope to that pool.</p>

